body:
it would be nicer to have 
```ts
test.describe('billing', { serial: true }, () => {
  /* ordered, shared state */
});
```

`existsSync` is also true for a directory, so a skill that `init` refused to install still gets a pointer. Repro: `mkdir -p .agents/skills/e2e/SKILL.md && e2e init --yes --mcp none --skills .agents/skills` prints `Broken, not touching: .agents/skills/e2e/ (SKILL.md is a directory)` and then `Created AGENTS.md`. The new line names a path an agent cannot read.

The same line misses one case. Start with a committed `.claude/skills/e2e -> ../../.agents/skills/e2e` link and no `.agents` copy. The first `init --yes` creates `AGENTS.md` only, and `CLAUDE.md` appears on the second run. `planSkillLink` plans nothing because the link already leads to the copy, and `SKILL.md` is not on disk yet.

This patch fixes both cases. Both repros now behave, and the 154 tests in the two test files still pass. oxlint asks for a `Set` for `goingPaths`.

```diff
-import { existsSync, lstatSync, readFileSync, type Stats } from 'node:fs';
+import { lstatSync, readFileSync, statSync, type Stats } from 'node:fs';
@@
+  const goingPaths = going.map((install) => onDiskPath(path.join(cwd, install.relative)));
   const held = skillDirs.filter((dir) =>
-    going.some((install) => install.relative === `${dir}/${SKILL_NAME}`) || existsSync(path.join(cwd, skillFile(dir))),
+    going.some((install) => install.relative === `${dir}/${SKILL_NAME}`)
+    || isFile(path.join(cwd, skillFile(dir)))
+    || goingPaths.includes(onDiskPath(path.join(cwd, dir, SKILL_NAME))),
   );
@@
+/** True when `target` is a file, following a final symlink. */
+function isFile(target: string): boolean {
+  try {
+    return statSync(target).isFile();
+  } catch {
+    return false;
+  }
+}
```

A symlinked instructions file is skipped here before its target is checked for the pointer. So an import of it never finds the pointer, and `CLAUDE.md` gets a second line. Repro: `shared.md` holds the pointer, `AGENTS.md -> shared.md`, and `CLAUDE.md` is `@AGENTS.md`. `init --yes` then prints `Updated CLAUDE.md` and appends the `.claude/skills` line. This conflicts with the new docs text in `cli.mdx` ("So does a `CLAUDE.md` that ... imports it ... with `@AGENTS.md`").

One reviewer tried this patch: the repro then leaves `CLAUDE.md` unchanged, a re-run prints `Nothing to create`, and the 154 tests pass. It needs a unit test for this layout.

```diff
-    if (entry?.isSymbolicLink()) continue;
+    if (entry?.isSymbolicLink()) {
+      const target = readOrUndefined(absolute);
+      if (target !== undefined && mentions.some((mention) => target.includes(mention))) pointed.add(onDiskPath(absolute));
+      continue;
+    }
```

Related, with the same kind of cause (`files` and `mentions` cover only this run's chosen directories):
- `--skills .claude/skills` with `CLAUDE.md` = `@AGENTS.md`, where `AGENTS.md` already has the pointer, appends a second pointer to `CLAUDE.md`.
- A `CLAUDE.md` that already names `.agents/skills/e2e/SKILL.md` gets the `.claude` line too when only `.claude/skills` is chosen.

Both are harmless duplicates, so they can go in a follow-up.

**P2 · Fix before merge · A customized built-in `<progress>` or `<meter>` still runs the page's `value` getter**

The tag check stops an autonomous custom element, but it does not stop a customized built-in. For `<progress is="upload-progress" value="1">`, the tag is `progress`, so `el.value` calls the page's override. An exact `getByLabel` reads every labelable candidate before it compares labels, so this getter runs during a lookup for an unrelated field.

The page for the probe:

```js
customElements.define('up-a', class extends HTMLProgressElement {
  get value() { throw new Error('upload not ready'); }
}, { extends: 'progress' });
document.body.innerHTML = '<input aria-label="Email" value="ada"><progress is="up-a" aria-label="Upload" value="1" max="10"></progress>';
```

| `screen.getByLabel('Email', { exact: true }).inputValue()` | Result |
| --- | --- |
| Merge base `3fec41f4b` | `"ada"` |
| This head `f22dddeb5` | `locate failed: locator.elementHandles: Error: upload not ready` |

The autonomous `<up-b role="progressbar">` from the new test gives `"ada"` on both sides. A getter that returns a number is a second form of the same problem: for `value="20" max="10"`, a getter that returns a percentage makes the read report `"100"`, not the clamped `"10"` that `expect.mdx` documents. The PR description says that a page's own `value` getter never runs, but that is not true for this case.

**P2 · Fix before merge · An instance of `class Object extends null` passes as a plain object**

**Trigger.** `browser.evaluate` returns an instance of a class named `Object` that extends `null`. Example, evaluated in a same-origin iframe:

```js
const C = class Object extends null {
  constructor() { return globalThis.Object.setPrototypeOf({ a: 1 }, new.target.prototype); }
  get total() { return 10; }
};
new C();
```

The prototype of the instance has a `null` prototype. Its own `constructor` is a function, the own `name` of that function is `'Object'`, and its own `prototype` points back. So `isObjectPrototype` returns `true`. The check does not use the realm, so an instance made in the evaluating frame passes the same way.

**Impact.** The class instance is encoded as JSON of its own data, and its prototype members are dropped without an error. The merge base rejected this value. The reference says that a class instance is not a `JsonValue` and gives `INVALID_ARGUMENT` (`docs/reference/web.mdx:671`). The PR description also says that a class instance, "a class named `Object` included", still fails. The only test of that claim is `new (class Object {})()` in `tests/unit/evaluation.test.ts`. That class does not extend `null`, so the check rejects it at the null-prototype test and the test does not reach the case above.

**Evidence.** I ran the example above through `browser.evaluate` in Chromium and WebKit:

| | merge base `970b74ed4` | this head `b8d45c006` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Chromium | `INVALID_ARGUMENT: evaluate result must be JSON-safe` | `{"a":1}` |
| WebKit | `INVALID_ARGUMENT: evaluate result must be JSON-safe` | `{"a":1}` |

**P2 · Fix before merge · A body read that the predicate starts and does not await is cancelled on the matched response too**

Every response view races its in-wait reads against `reads`, and `reads` includes `waited.signal`. The `finally` block always aborts `waited`, so it also cancels a read that is still pending on the response that the wait returns. `settle()` sets `settled = true`, but a read that started before `settle()` is already in `raceAbort(start(), waitSignal, ...)` and gets the abort.

Trigger: a predicate that starts `r.text()` without `await` and returns `true`, on a response whose body has not finished:

```ts
let pending!: Promise<string>;
const matched = await browser.waitForResponse((r) => {
  if (!r.url.endsWith('/api/slow')) return false;
  pending = r.text();
  return true;
});
await pending;        // rejects: CANCELLED "waitForResponse body cancelled"
await matched.text(); // resolves: "slow-body"
```

I ran this against the head build: `pending` rejected with `CANCELLED`, and a later `matched.text()` on the same response returned the body. Without a `.catch`, the floating read is an unhandled rejection.

The comment at lines 958-959 and `docs/reference/web.mdx:905-906` say that only the reads on passed-over responses are cancelled.

**P3 · Non-blocking · The read types of the public config interfaces are wider**

_Optional. This does not block the merge._

`TargetApp.permissions`, `AgentOptions.providerOptions` (`:1967`), `tools`, `agents`, `credentials`, and `secrets` now allow `undefined` values. Code that reads these fields from an `E2EConfig` or `AgentConfig` and passes them on to a strict type stops compiling. For example:

```ts
function forward(agent: AgentConfig): Record<string, Record<string, unknown>> | undefined {
  return agent.providerOptions; // TS2322 on this head, OK on main
}
function perms(config: E2EConfig): Readonly<Record<string, 'grant' | 'deny' | 'reset'>> | 'all' | undefined {
  return config.targets[0]?.app?.permissions; // TS2322 on this head, OK on main
}
```

`tsc --strict --module nodenext` against the built packages: 0 errors on main, 2 errors on this head. `CONTRIBUTING.md` lists "widening a returned type" as a breaking change to the public types. The trigger is uncommon, because user code seldom reads these fields back from the config.

**P2 · Fix before merge · Exported callback types no longer accept code that compiles on main**

The new required info parameter and the `TestInfo` default break three existing uses of exported types. Each of these compiles against main and fails against this head (strict `tsc`, same consumer file):

```ts
import { test } from 'e2e';
import type { FixtureFn, TestFn, TestHookFn, WorkerFixtures } from 'e2e';

const workerThing: FixtureFn<WorkerFixtures, string> = async ({}, use) => { await use('x'); };
test.extend<{}, { thing: string }>({ thing: [workerThing, { scope: 'worker' }] }); // TS2322

export function wrap(body: TestFn): TestFn {
  return async (fixtures) => { await body(fixtures); }; // TS2554: Expected 2 arguments
}

const suiteSetup: TestHookFn<WorkerFixtures> = async () => {};
test.beforeAll(suiteSetup); // TS2345
```

- `FixtureFn<Fixtures, Value>` (`packages/core/src/types/fixtures.ts:51`) now defaults `Info` to `TestInfo`, so a worker fixture typed this way is not assignable where `FixtureFn<..., WorkerInfo>` is expected.
- `TestFn` (`packages/core/src/types.ts:951`) now requires a second argument at every call site.
- `TestHookFn<WorkerFixtures>` defaults `Info` to `TestInfo`, so `beforeAll` and `afterAll` reject it. On main, the `@deprecated` note on `SuiteHookFn` tells users to write exactly this type.

The changeset is a `patch`. AGENTS.md requires a breaking change to be a `minor` whose summary starts with `**Breaking:**`. The type tests in `packages/e2e/tests/types/sdk-types.ts` do not cover these three forms.

**P2 · Fix before merge · test.info() from a timed-out body returns the next test's TestInfo**

`currentTestInfo()` reads the process-wide published attempt and does not check whether the caller's phase has ended. A body that continues after its timeout therefore gets the `TestInfo` of whichever attempt runs next.

Repro (one worker, fake engine, built head):

```ts
globalThis.__gate ??= (() => { let release; const p = new Promise((r) => { release = r; }); return { p, release }; })();
test('first', { timeout: 300 }, async () => {
  await globalThis.__gate.p;
  test.info().annotations.push({ type: 'leaked-from', description: 'first' });
});
test('second', async () => {
  globalThis.__gate.release();
  await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 200));
});
```

Result: `first` is `timed-out`, `test.info().title` in its continuation is `second`, and `report.json` shows `{ type: 'leaked-from', description: 'first' }` on the `second` result. The same continuation can also write into the next test's `outputPath()` and read its `status`.

`test.step()` refuses this case with `CANCELLED` through `leftRunning()` (`packages/core/src/internal/test-step.ts`). `test.info()` does not.
